WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.520
There’s this scene in the movie Arrival
where Jeremy Renner’s character, who's a scientist,

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.950
is brought in to try to help the military
deal with alien spaceships that have landed

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.280
across the globe.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.600
And in an early version of the script, he asks
if the aliens have responded to Fourier series

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.289
sent out by the humans.

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:19.099
Problem is, that wasn’t really the right
math reference.

00:00:19.099 --> 00:00:24.890
“Well, a Fourier series is a mathematical
concept, but it's not, like, a series of numbers

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:30.009
so I, I changed that to Fibonacci series — which
is in fact a series of numbers that aliens

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:33.100
might actually send us as a way of getting
our attention.”

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.829
Phil is an astronomer and science blogger,
and he’s also part of the Science and Entertainment

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:40.890
Exchange, an organization that connects creators
in Hollywood with scientist script advisors.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.890
They set up a phone number for screenwriters
like Arrival’s Eric Heisserer, who want

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:46.980
their scientific references to sound right.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.940
“Here’s one of the things I wanted to
make sure to do with this movie — and that’s

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:58.129
not to be embarrassed about complicated ideas,
not to be embarrassed or ashamed of hard

00:00:58.129 --> 00:00:59.370
science.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.080
That these are the words and the terms that
people in this position would be sharing

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.080
with each other.”

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.330
Phil’s advice to use “fibonacci” added
that extra bit of credibility.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:14.232
“And a I didn't really think much of that
but then you know, I go see the movie, and

00:01:14.232 --> 00:01:16.300
Jeremy Renner actually says that….

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.220
“Have they responded to anything — shapes,
patterns, numbers, Fibonacci?”

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:25.320
“… and I'm nudging my wife like, pointing,
pointing to the screen, pointing, like yeah,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.250
he said what I wrote, that's so cool!”

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.120
There’s a lot of really bad science in TV
and movies.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.120
“I’m getting hacked!”

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.580
There’s bad computer science, bad geology,
bad physics.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.570
So in 2008, the Office of Communications at
the National Academy of Sciences launched

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.150
the Science and Entertainment Exchange.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:46.300
Here’s how it works: you call the number,
you describe your question, and then the team

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:51.640
filters through their database of 2,400 scientists
and engineers to put you in touch with the

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.640
right person.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.260
They estimate that they’ve done between
1700 and 1800 consultations since they started.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.230
That includes almost every Marvel movie, and
two 2017 Oscar nominees.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.080
A lot of times, the consultation boils down
to changing a line to include a scientific

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.080
concept.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.050
In early discussions for the 2011 Thor movie,
producer Kevin Feige needed a way to represent

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.600
the “rainbow bridge” that reaches between
Earth and the realm of the Gods in Norse mythology.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.820
So he met with Sean Carroll, a physics professor,
who later paraphrased their conversation in

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:19.890
a blog post.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.130
Carroll suggested they describe the bridge
as wormhole.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.760
Feige didn’t want to do that, because it
sounded too Nineties.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.770
MERCHANT: And he said, “Well, there is this
thing called the Einstein-Rosen bridge.”

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.440
“Einstein-Rosen bridge?

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.319
What is that?”

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:35.700
Einstein-Rosen Bridge is the technical name
for a wormhole.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.750
And they said “We LOVE Einstein-Rosen bridge.”

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.060
Listen to the dialogue in the movie, and you
can hear where they added in the concept of

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.760
an Einstein-Rosen Bridge.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.180
"An Einstein-Rosen Bridge is a theoretical connection between two different points of space-time."

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:48.620
"It's a wormhole."

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.620
But the team’s focus isn’t just about
what characters are saying — it’s also

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:53.980
about who is saying them.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:59.819
In the original Thor comics, Natalie Portman’s
character — Jane Foster — is a nurse.

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:03.610
The physics professor who consulted the filmmakers
argued that it would make more sense for

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:07.450
the plot if she was a physicist studying the
wormhole that brought Thor to Earth.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:12.010
And for the Exchange, that sounded like the
perfect opportunity to break down gender stereotypes.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:16.550
“We love nurses, but we also really love
the idea of a strong female character who

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.540
was an astrophysicist.”

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.970
So the writers changed it.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.420
“You're an astrophysicist, not some storm
chaser.”

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.420
"I'm telling you, there's a connection."

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.561
Two years later, that character was the focal
point of a contest hosted by Marvel and the

00:03:28.561 --> 00:03:31.720
Science and Entertainment Exchange, where
young women interested in STEM careers could

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.960
interview female scientists in the Exchange’s
database for a chance to tour laboratories

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.180
and film sets.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:42.930
“We are very concerned with the way that
scientists are portrayed onscreen.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.740
We want them to be — I mean, you always
want them to be the hero, that’s the best

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.560
thing — and a main character is fantastic.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.849
But we want to see women, we want to see people
of color, and we want to see them woven into

00:03:55.849 --> 00:04:00.989
the fabric of the everyday life that you see
onscreen.”

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:05.430
Since 1983, repeated studies have shown that
when children are asked to draw a scientist,

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.330
they overwhelmingly draw old, white men.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:12.790
Children usually cite film or cartoon characters
as their main source of inspiration.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.409
In the original research, children drew these
stereotypical characteristics more and more

00:04:16.409 --> 00:04:18.289
frequently as they got older.

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:20.539
Science has had another image issue in Hollywood,
too.

00:04:20.539 --> 00:04:25.479
A survey of over a thousand horror movies
distributed in Britain between 1931 and 1984

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:28.990
found that scientists and their work were
the villains of 41 percent of films.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:32.630
Scientists were heroes less than 1 percent
of the time.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:35.620
In the decades since then, perceptions of
science have gotten better.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.490
An analysis of over 3,000 interviews on public
opinion about scientists found that American

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:44.949
adults in 2001 were much less likely to hold
negative stereotypes about scientists than

00:04:44.949 --> 00:04:46.919
they were in 1983.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:50.330
And they were much more likely to believe
that a science career was a good choice for

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:51.940
their children or for themselves.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.979
“I mean, people do learn from film and television.

00:04:54.979 --> 00:04:57.199
Whether you want them to or not.

00:04:57.199 --> 00:04:59.159
We often refer to it as an ‘accidental curriculum.’”

00:04:59.159 --> 00:05:03.990
One of the most talked-about examples of that
is what’s called the CSI Effect — the

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.969
idea that chart-topping crime shows increased
interest in forensic science, but also spread

00:05:07.969 --> 00:05:09.589
a lot of misinformation about 
the field.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.330
CSI didn't just launch a franchise, it created a false impression when it comes to solving crimes.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:19.229
Researchers in 2010 found that watching the
show is associated with perceptions of DNA

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:22.319
evidence as much more reliable than it actually
is.

00:05:22.319 --> 00:05:26.139
The Exchange wants to mobilize that kind of
effect — and hopefully make science more

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:29.139
understandable and more accessible for everyone.

